WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.135
New details about that second

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.336
grand jury

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.237
that's been meeting in the Trump

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:06.272
classified documents investigation.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:06.873
Sources say that

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.241
this jury, which is based in

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:09.542
southern Florida,

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.710
has heard testimony

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.945
from multiple witnesses

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:12.645
in recent weeks

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:13.980
and is set to hear

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.782
from at least two more witnesses.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:16.883
Let's get straight to CNN's

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:17.183
Kristen

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.119
Holmes is breaking this for us Kristen.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:20.687
So this is in the same jurisdiction

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:21.988
where the search warrant for Mar

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:24.057
a Lago was approved. Tell us more.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:24.457
That's right.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:25.792
So what we know so far

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:26.626
is that this grand jury

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:27.260
has been meeting

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.895
in a courthouse in Miami,

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:29.963
and they have recently heard,

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:30.663
as you said,

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.232
from a number of witnesses,

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:33.266
they are expected to hear

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:34.434
from another witness

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:35.368
in this investigation

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.771
into the classified probes tomorrow.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.606
And after that, at least

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:40.740
one more witness.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.476
Now, what's not clear is why exactly

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.311
they have this second grand jury.

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:46.579
And I talked to a number of sources

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:47.514
close to Trump

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.616
who said that they were also unsure

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:51.384
why this grand jury was convening,

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.253
why they were hearing the case.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.788
One thing to note is that Jack

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:55.822
Smith has the authority

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:56.723
as a special counsel

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.092
to operate and bring charges

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.660
in any jurisdiction.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.762
So this is completely within his purview.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.564
Now, as you mentioned,

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:05.598
there was the person,

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:06.966
the court that signed off

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.701
on that search warrant for Mar

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:09.035
a Lago

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:11.137
was in the Southern District in Florida.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.205
The other thing about that

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:12.906
it's interesting

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:15.008
is the US, an assistant U.S.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.910
attorney from the Miami

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.078
bureau, was present

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.647
when that search was executed in Mar

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:21.247
a Lago.

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:22.549
And one thing to bring up here

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.184
is that there have been questions

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:25.552
about venue

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:26.986
since before the special counsel

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.421
even took over this investigation

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.223
because it dealt with taking documents

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:31.357
from the White House

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.526
to Trump's Florida Florida resort.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.328
So that has always been up.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.097
But again, it's still unclear

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:38.798
why exactly.

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:39.732
They they are

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.501
have convened the second grand jury, why

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:42.802
they have our hearing

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:43.136
excuse me,

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:44.337
why they have bringing witnesses

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:45.805
in front of the second grand jury, Jake.

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:46.172
All right.

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:47.640
Kristen Holmes with that breaking news

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.241
Thanks so much.

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:48.675
Let's bring in

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:49.642
CNN senior legal

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:50.910
analyst and former

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.611
federal prosecutor

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.546
Ali Hornick to talk about this Ali.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:54.247
Up until now,

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.049
we've only known of the one

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.150
grand jury in the Trump

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:57.851
documents case,

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:59.452
the grand jury here in Washington, D.C.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.020
But now we're hearing more

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.222
because of our great reporters

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:03.556
about multiple witnesses

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.159
testifying to a grand jury in Florida.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.261
What could that mean for this case?

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:08.728
Well, Jake, it's

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:10.063
a really interesting development.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.198
I see two broad possibilities.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:12.632
One is

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.400
this could be a convenience measure

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.835
if there are certain witnesses

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.070
who can't or won't travel

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:17.871
outside of Florida.

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.139
What you can do as a prosecutor

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:20.507
is take their testimony

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:22.142
in a federal grand jury down there

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:23.176
and then take the transcripts

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.711
and just read it to your main grand

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.179
jury in D.C..

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:28.181
The other possibility is prosecutors

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.282
could be examining

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.451
the possibility of charging this case

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.086
down in the southern district of Florida.

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:34.988
They would have the ability to do that.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.756
Prosecutors, when you have a crime,

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:37.724
you have to charge it in

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:38.758
one of the districts

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.026
where some of the criminal

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:40.927
conduct occurred.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.962
You do have some leeway as a prosecutor,

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:43.963
but you have to get it right.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:45.165
If you charged in the wrong district,

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:46.900
the case will get thrown out.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.901
So theoretically,

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:51.037
could it be some sort of criminal charge?

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:52.539
And I'm just speculating here.

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:53.773
So but some sort of criminal charge

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:56.943
having to do with holding the documents

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.612
improperly in Florida

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.514
and then the obstruction of justice

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.216
charge in Washington, D.C.,

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:04.684
something like that, would there.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:05.351
Would they charge

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.653
in two different jurisdictions

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:07.720
at the same time?

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.255
Theoretically, you can do that,

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.723
but I do not think prosecutors

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:11.124
would do that.

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:12.225
That would be a practical

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.293
and logistical mess.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.460
You then have two cases

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.328
in two different courtrooms.

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.096
You'd have to have to trials

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:18.231
going on at the same time.

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.766
I think prosecutors are going to have

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:21.067
to essentially decide,

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.869
are we charging this in D.C. or Florida?

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:24.470
Now, the advantage to D.C.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.339
for prosecutors, let's just be real here

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.141
Donald Trump is very unpopular.

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:31.110
He got 5.4% of the vote in D.C.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:31.778
in 20, 20.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:33.513
The jury pool is going to be way better

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.114
for prosecutors in D.C.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.784
But venue is clearer to me in Florida

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:39.219
because that's where the documents

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:39.686
were kept, that

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:41.354
certainly where the obstructive

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:42.155
acts occurred.

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:44.090
And so if they charge in D.C.,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.124
they do run the risk of

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.859
not having the proper venue here,

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.761
which is a very high stakes risk.

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:50.496
Although you could argue

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:51.965
that if they get

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:53.333
an indictment in Florida,

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:55.468
it will have more credibility

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:57.003
because that's a state

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.072
that Donald Trump won twice.

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:00.006
We know

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:00.807
Trump's lawyers met

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:01.674
with Justice Department

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.209
officials yesterday.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.111
The defense lawyers ever succeed

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:07.380
in changing prosecutors minds

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.348
when they have these meetings,

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:09.115
they're obviously trying

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.216
to convince them

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:13.353
to not pursue an indictment against Mr.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:13.987
Trump.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.122
I would say rarely, but not never.

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:16.923
There are times

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.224
when prosecutors already have

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.092
their minds made up.

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:20.627
But you still give the defense lawyer

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:22.595
the meeting as a professional courtesy.

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:23.596
But there are cases

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:24.697
where it's a close call

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:26.733
where a powerful, persuasive,

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:28.401
substantive presentation

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:28.901
by defense

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.670
lawyers can convince prosecutors

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.338
not to charge or to charge

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.473
less serious conduct.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:33.873
I'll tell you,

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:34.841
there were times

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.809
when I was persuaded as a prosecutor,

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:37.910
but if I had to put a number on,

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.278
I would say

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:38.911
it's in the low

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.647
single digit of percentages.

00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:41.881
All right, Leonnig, thanks so much.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:42.448
Appreciate it.

